1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,172
BELOW THE EMBER
An AI educational game by Bekzat Zhailkhan

2
00:00:10,172 --> 00:00:25,296
The last keeper carries an ember into forgotten ruins.
The English prologue uses animated scenes.

3
00:00:25,296 --> 00:00:33,642
A normal seeded run: movement, dash and four weapons.

4
00:00:33,642 --> 00:00:41,988
Attacks use real projectiles, health and cooldowns.

5
00:00:41,988 --> 00:00:50,334
This short run includes a real defeat.
It is not a full campaign victory.

6
00:00:50,334 --> 00:00:58,725
Week 3: CSP assigns rooms and checks their separation.

7
00:00:58,725 --> 00:01:07,117
Invalid room counts produce a clear message.
The previous map stays unchanged.

8
00:01:07,117 --> 00:01:15,508
Search limits can trigger a fallback layout.
The laboratory labels the fallback and truncated trace.

9
00:01:15,508 --> 00:01:28,022
Week 2: A* searches around walls.
Users can change obstacles, the start and the goal.

10
00:01:28,022 --> 00:01:40,536
The search chooses the smallest f = g + h.
Open and closed nodes show its progress.

11
00:01:40,536 --> 00:01:44,619
A* result: 30 moves and 165 closed nodes.

12
00:01:44,619 --> 00:01:51,287
Week 1: an enemy senses the player and changes state.

13
00:01:51,287 --> 00:01:57,955
Detection can lead to alert, chase and attack.

14
00:01:57,955 --> 00:02:04,623
After losing sight, the agent searches, then patrols.

15
00:02:04,623 --> 00:02:12,204
Extra topic: Q-learning starts with an empty table.

16
00:02:12,204 --> 00:02:19,786
1000 episodes: the learned policy reaches the goal.
98% success refers to recent training on this fixed map.

17
00:02:19,786 --> 00:02:24,786
Three core weeks: Agents, A* and CSP.
TypeScript, Phaser 3 and Vite.

18
00:02:24,786 --> 00:02:29,786
50 tests pass. A separate automated driver completed five floors.
The driver result is not a manual playthrough.
